NNAAMMEE
     ddhhccrreellaayy66 — Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol for IPv6 (DHCPv6) relay
     agent

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     ddhhccrreellaayy66 [--ddlloovv] [--EE _e_n_t_e_r_p_r_i_s_e_-_n_u_m_b_e_r] [--II _i_n_t_e_r_f_a_c_e_-_i_d] [--RR _r_e_m_o_t_e_-_i_d]
               --ii _i_n_t_e_r_f_a_c_e _d_e_s_t_i_n_a_t_i_o_n _._._.

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ddhhccrreellaayy66 utility provides a means for relaying DHCPv6 requests from
     a subnet to which no DHCP server is directly connected to one or more
     DHCPv6 servers on other subnets.

     ddhhccrreellaayy66 listens for DHCPv6 requests on a given interface.  When a query
     is received, ddhhccrreellaayy66 forwards it to the list of DHCP destinations spec‐
     ified on the command line.  When a reply is received, it is sent on the
     network from whence the original request came.

     The _d_e_s_t_i_n_a_t_i_o_n might be an address followed by a `%' and an interface
     name, or just an interface name (e.g. "2001:db8::1%em0" or "em1").  When
     no address is specified, ddhhccrreellaayy66 will use multicast on the specified
     interface.

     The options are as follows:

     --dd      Do not daemonize.  If this option is specified, ddhhccrreellaayy66 will
             run in the foreground and log to _s_t_d_e_r_r.

     --EE _e_n_t_e_r_p_r_i_s_e_-_n_u_m_b_e_r
             Choose the _e_n_t_e_r_p_r_i_s_e_-_n_u_m_b_e_r that will be used by the Remote-ID
             option (this only has effect when using --RR).

     --II _i_n_t_e_r_f_a_c_e_-_i_d
             The _i_n_t_e_r_f_a_c_e_-_i_d relay agent information option value that
             ddhhccrreellaayy66 should use on relayed packets.  If this option is not
             specified, it will use the interface name by default.

             Avoid using this option when using Lightweight DHCPv6 Relay Mode
             (layer 2 relay), otherwise ddhhccrreellaayy66 will always send replies
             back to the client interface, which will break networks with mul‐
             tiple DHCPv6 layer 2 relay agents.

     --ii _i_n_t_e_r_f_a_c_e
             The name of the network interface which will receive client
             DHCPv6 requests.  For layer 3 mode at least one IPv6 local, site
             or global address has to be configured on this interface.

     --ll      Use the Lightweight DHCPv6 Relay Agent mode (layer 2 relaying).

     --oo      Add the Interface-ID option.  This option is activated by default
             when using layer 2 relaying.

     --RR _r_e_m_o_t_e_-_i_d
             Enable and add the specified Relay Agent _r_e_m_o_t_e_-_i_d to identify
             this relay segment.

     --vv      Show verbose messages.  Implies --dd.

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
     Relay multicast packets in the current network to a unicast address (the
     relay must have a global address in em0):

           # dhcrelay6 -i em0 2001:db8::1000%em0

     Listen to one subnet and multicast DHCPv6 packets to another (requires at
     least link-local addresses):

           # dhcrelay6 -i em0 em1

     Relay DHCPv6 packets with Interface-ID (option 18) using the input inter‐
     face as its content:

           # dhcrelay6 -o -i em0 2001:db8::1000%em0

     Same thing as before but with a custom Interface-ID:

           # dhcrelay6 -o -I "OpenBSD Router 1" -i em0 2001:db8::1000%em0

     Use Lightweight DHCPv6 Relay Agent (layer 2 relay) in a bridged or
     switched network (no IPv6 address required).  Only makes sense when em0
     and em1 are configured in a bridge(4), since ddhhccrreellaayy66 needs to drop the
     original DHCPv6 packets and send modified ones with Interface-ID option.

           # dhcrelay6 -l -i em0 em1

     Identify a segment using Lightweight DHCPv6 Relay Agent (layer 2 relay)
     with a Remote-ID (option 37) instead of an Interface-ID:

           # dhcrelay6 -l -R "OpenBSD Router A" -i em0 em1

